{"text": " A treasury analyst at a Nairobi bank noticed something odd. Empessa reversals in one branch were occurring at exactly three times the rate of every other branch. Manual review would have taken six weeks to find this in 400,000 transactions. An AI prompt found it in eight minutes. The branch manager had been approving fictitious reversals. The loss to that point was 3.4 million Kenyan shillings. In this lesson pattern find in asks what is the trend and what breaks the trend. Anomaly detection asks which data points fall outside the expected range and what do they indicate. Together they transform raw data into early warning systems and opportunity signals. The two things every Kenyan business decision maker needs most. We cover the four types of patterns every business analyst must be able to identify. Trend, seasonal, cyclical and irregular. How to write AI prompts that separate normal variation from meaningful patterns. Anomaly detection defining your expected range and flagging deviations and the Kenyan business calendar understanding seasonal patterns specific to the East African market. Business data contains four types of patterns. Trend is a long run increase or decrease over time. Monthly revenue growing 8% quarter on quarter. Seasonal patterns repeat on a predictable calendar cycle. Empessa transaction volumes spike in January as school fees are paid. Drop in August during agricultural offseason peak again in December. The Kenyan business calendar has patterns that standard global analytics tools do not know about. School fee payment months January September. Salary payment dates 25th to 31st of month. Agricultural seasons, long rains march to May, short rains October to December and public holiday clusters all create predictable patterns in East African business data. Build these into every pattern analysis prompt as context and your AI output will be far more accurate than any generic analysis tool. Remember this, never report an anomaly without a hypothesis about its cause. A spike or drop without an explanation is not a finding. It is a starting point for investigation. The pattern tells you where to look. The investigation tells you what actually happened. Apply what you learn in this lesson and you will see results immediately. Your skills bear fruit.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 5.38, "text": " A treasury analyst at a Nairobi bank noticed something odd.", "tokens": [50364, 316, 47213, 19085, 412, 257, 50205, 20027, 3765, 5694, 746, 7401, 13, 50672], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18383894393693156, "compression_ratio": 1.435897435897436, "no_speech_prob": 0.008783590979874134, "words": [{"word": " A", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.22, "probability": 0.9396454691886902}, {"word": " treasury", "start": 0.22, "end": 0.72, "probability": 0.3019348382949829}, {"word": " analyst", "start": 0.72, "end": 1.6, "probability": 0.9714265465736389}, {"word": " at", "start": 1.6, "end": 1.92, "probability": 0.9936708211898804}, {"word": " a", "start": 1.92, "end": 2.08, "probability": 0.9239135980606079}, {"word": " Nairobi", "start": 2.08, "end": 2.6, "probability": 0.9753645956516266}, {"word": " bank", "start": 2.6, "end": 3.28, "probability": 0.8137215971946716}, {"word": " noticed", "start": 3.28, "end": 4.22, "probability": 0.8870938420295715}, {"word": " something", "start": 4.22, "end": 4.92, "probability": 0.9929814338684082}, {"word": " odd.", "start": 4.92, "end": 5.38, "probability": 0.998833954334259}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 6.36, "end": 12.54, "text": " Empessa reversals in one branch were occurring at exactly three times the rate", "tokens": [50672, 8599, 8391, 14582, 1124, 294, 472, 9819, 645, 18386, 412, 2293, 1045, 1413, 264, 3314, 51012], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18383894393693156, "compression_ratio": 1.435897435897436, "no_speech_prob": 0.008783590979874134, "words": [{"word": " Empessa", "start": 6.36, "end": 6.86, "probability": 0.222131483256